// Kotlin Pointer to work with the live Rust struct on the other side of the FFI.
//
// There's some subtlety here, because we have to be careful not to operate on a Rust
// struct after it has been dropped, and because we must expose a public API for freeing
// theq Kotlin wrapper object in lieu of reliable finalizers. The core requirements are:
//
//   * Each instance holds an opaque pointer to the underlying Rust struct.
//     Method calls need to read this pointer from the object's state and pass it in to
//     the Rust FFI.
//
//   * When an instance is no longer needed, its pointer should be passed to a
//     special destructor function provided by the Rust FFI, which will drop the
//     underlying Rust struct.
//
//   * Given an instance, calling code is expected to call the special
//     `destroy` method in order to free it after use, either by calling it explicitly
//     or by using a higher-level helper like the `use` method. Failing to do so risks
//     leaking the underlying Rust struct.
//
//   * We can't assume that calling code will do the right thing, and must be prepared
//     to handle Kotlin method calls executing concurrently with or even after a call to
//     `destroy`, and to handle multiple (possibly concurrent!) calls to `destroy`.
//
//   * We must never allow Rust code to operate on the underlying Rust struct after
//     the destructor has been called, and must never call the destructor more than once.
//     Doing so may trigger memory unsafety.
//
//   * To mitigate many of the risks of leaking memory and use-after-free unsafety, a `Cleaner`
//     is implemented to call the destructor when the Kotlin object becomes unreachable.
//     This is done in a background thread. This is not a panacea, and client code should be aware that
//      1. the thread may starve if some there are objects that have poorly performing
//     `drop` methods or do significant work in their `drop` methods.
//      2. the thread is shared across the whole library. This can be tuned by using `android_cleaner = true`,
//         or `android = true` in the [`kotlin` section of the `uniffi.toml` file](https://mozilla.github.io/uniffi-rs/kotlin/configuration.html).
//
// If we try to implement this with mutual exclusion on access to the pointer, there is the
// possibility of a race between a method call and a concurrent call to `destroy`:
//
//    * Thread A starts a method call, reads the value of the pointer, but is interrupted
//      before it can pass the pointer over the FFI to Rust.
//    * Thread B calls `destroy` and frees the underlying Rust struct.
//    * Thread A resumes, passing the already-read pointer value to Rust and triggering
//      a use-after-free.
//
// One possible solution would be to use a `ReadWriteLock`, with each method call taking
// a read lock (and thus allowed to run concurrently) and the special `destroy` method
// taking a write lock (and thus blocking on live method calls). However, we aim not to
// generate methods with any hidden blocking semantics, and a `destroy` method that might
// block if called incorrectly seems to meet that bar.
//
// So, we achieve our goals by giving each instance an associated `AtomicLong` counter to track
// the number of in-flight method calls, and an `AtomicBoolean` flag to indicate whether `destroy`
// has been called. These are updated according to the following rules:
//
//    * The initial value of the counter is 1, indicating a live object with no in-flight calls.
//      The initial value for the flag is false.
//
//    * At the start of each method call, we atomically check the counter.
//      If it is 0 then the underlying Rust struct has already been destroyed and the call is aborted.
//      If it is nonzero them we atomically increment it by 1 and proceed with the method call.
//
//    * At the end of each method call, we atomically decrement and check the counter.
//      If it has reached zero then we destroy the underlying Rust struct.
//
//    * When `destroy` is called, we atomically flip the flag from false to true.
//      If the flag was already true we silently fail.
//      Otherwise we atomically decrement and check the counter.
//      If it has reached zero then we destroy the underlying Rust struct.
//
// Astute readers may observe that this all sounds very similar to the way that Rust's `Arc<T>` works,
// and indeed it is, with the addition of a flag to guard against multiple calls to `destroy`.
//
// The overall effect is that the underlying Rust struct is destroyed only when `destroy` has been
// called *and* all in-flight method calls have completed, avoiding violating any of the expectations
// of the underlying Rust code.
//
// This makes a cleaner a better alternative to _not_ calling `destroy()` as
// and when the object is finished with, but the abstraction is not perfect: if the Rust object's `drop`
// method is slow, and/or there are many objects to cleanup, and it's on a low end Android device, then the cleaner
// thread may be starved, and the app will leak memory.
//
// In this case, `destroy`ing manually may be a better solution.
//
// The cleaner can live side by side with the manual calling of `destroy`. In the order of responsiveness, uniffi objects
// with Rust peers are reclaimed:
//
// 1. By calling the `destroy` method of the object, which calls `rustObject.free()`. If that doesn't happen:
// 2. When the object becomes unreachable, AND the Cleaner thread gets to call `rustObject.free()`. If the thread is starved then:
// 3. The memory is reclaimed when the process terminates.
//
// [1] https://stackoverflow.com/questions/24376768/can-java-finalize-an-object-when-it-is-still-in-scope/24380219
//


/**
 * Root handle to the Streamify core engine.
 *
 * Thread-safe by construction: interior state is shared behind
 * `Arc` + `RwLock`/atomics, so the handle can be cloned into any
 * Kotlin coroutine dispatcher or thread without external locking.
 *
 * Every method is `[Throws=EngineError]` (Phase 3 / MP-C06): all
 * entrypoints are panic-shielded, and a panic anywhere under a method
 * surfaces as `EngineError.Internal { error_message, backtrace }` instead
 * of
 * unwinding across the FFI boundary.
 */
public interface StreamifyEngineInterface {
    
    /**
     * Register a push event listener (MP-C02). The registration itself
     * bumps the revision, so every listener — including the fresh one —
     * receives a full snapshot on the dispatcher's next pass. Returns
     * the number of registered listeners after the call.
     */
    fun `addEventListener`(`listener`: EngineEventListener): kotlin.UInt
    
    /**
     * Atomically apply one Jam CRDT delta batch: `delta` is a
     * concatenation of 48-byte little-endian wire ops (the frozen
     * `rust_48` image pinned by fixtures/jam_golden_vectors.json). The
     * whole batch merges under one lock acquisition; corrupt rows are
     * counted in `ops_rejected` and skipped, never aborting the batch.
     * A length that is not a multiple of 48 fails as Internal.
     */
    fun `applyJamDelta`(`delta`: kotlin.ByteArray): JamSyncResult
    
    /**
     * Create the engine's active Jam session (UFFI-03/04). Creating
     * while a session is live replaces it with a fresh, empty replica
     * (the engine is the room's local mirror; a new room means a new
     * mirror). A blank or oversized name is rejected as Internal.
     */
    fun `createJamSession`(`sessionName`: kotlin.String): JamSessionHandle
    
    /**
     * Current buffering health snapshot (one-shot read).
     */
    fun `getBufferStats`(): BufferStats
    
    /**
     * Latest coalesced engine snapshot (the same image pushed to
     * listeners; one-shot read for cold-start UI hydration).
     */
    fun `getEngineSnapshot`(): EngineSnapshot
    
    /**
     * Deterministic BLAKE3 state hash (64 hex chars) of the active
     * session's canonical CRDT fold. Requires a live session.
     */
    fun `getJamStateHash`(): kotlin.String
    
    /**
     * Current playback lifecycle state (one-shot read; UI shells should
     * prefer the push channel via `add_event_listener` — MP-DEC-04).
     */
    fun `getPlaybackState`(): PlaybackState
    
    /**
     * Arm the engine: verify runtime environment invariants, then
     * advance the lifecycle one-way Idle -> Buffering. Idempotent;
     * a progressed state is never pulled back.
     */
    fun `initialize`()
    
    /**
     * Feed one reverse-plane media command (MP-C03). Applies the
     * mandated 50-150 ms hardware debounce: an IDENTICAL command
     * (same variant, same payload) arriving inside the window is
     * swallowed. Returns true when applied, false when debounced.
     */
    fun `onMediaCommand`(`command`: MediaCommand): kotlin.Boolean
    
    /**
     * Feed one live telemetry sample from the playback buffer and the
     * acoustic pipeline. Malformed samples (NaN/±inf fill ratio) are
     * rejected; out-of-range fill ratios clamp to [0.0, 1.0].
     */
    fun `recordBufferStats`(`bufferedDurationMs`: kotlin.ULong, `underrunCount`: kotlin.UInt, `fillRatio`: kotlin.Float)
    
    /**
     * Record one hardware DAC presentation clock sample (MP-C01). The
     * platform HAL adapter calls this from its callback (never the
     * engine's audio thread). The sample is validated (rate within the
     * supported DAC range) and republished in every pushed snapshot.
     */
    fun `recordDacClock`(`clock`: DacPresentationClock)
    
    /**
     * Apply the output gain scalar. Clamped to [0.0, 1.0]; a NaN input
     * fails safe to full mute. Stored lock-free (Release/Acquire).
     */
    fun `setVolume`(`volume`: kotlin.Float)
    
    companion object
}

/**
 * Root handle to the Streamify core engine.
 *
 * Thread-safe by construction: interior state is shared behind
 * `Arc` + `RwLock`/atomics, so the handle can be cloned into any
 * Kotlin coroutine dispatcher or thread without external locking.
 *
 * Every method is `[Throws=EngineError]` (Phase 3 / MP-C06): all
 * entrypoints are panic-shielded, and a panic anywhere under a method
 * surfaces as `EngineError.Internal { error_message, backtrace }` instead
 * of
 * unwinding across the FFI boundary.
 */
open class StreamifyEngine: Disposable, AutoCloseable, StreamifyEngineInterface {

    constructor(pointer: Pointer) {
        this.pointer = pointer
        this.cleanable = UniffiLib.CLEANER.register(this, UniffiCleanAction(pointer))
    }

    /**
     * This constructor can be used to instantiate a fake object. Only used for tests. Any
     * attempt to actually use an object constructed this way will fail as there is no
     * connected Rust object.
     */
    @Suppress("UNUSED_PARAMETER")
    constructor(noPointer: NoPointer) {
        this.pointer = null
        this.cleanable = UniffiLib.CLEANER.register(this, UniffiCleanAction(pointer))
    }
    constructor() :
        this(
    uniffiRustCall() { _status ->
    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_constructor_streamifyengine_new(
        _status)
}
    )

    protected val pointer: Pointer?
    protected val cleanable: UniffiCleaner.Cleanable

    private val wasDestroyed = AtomicBoolean(false)
    private val callCounter = AtomicLong(1)

    override fun destroy() {
        // Only allow a single call to this method.
        // TODO: maybe we should log a warning if called more than once?
        if (this.wasDestroyed.compareAndSet(false, true)) {
            // This decrement always matches the initial count of 1 given at creation time.
            if (this.callCounter.decrementAndGet() == 0L) {
                cleanable.clean()
            }
        }
    }

    @Synchronized
    override fun close() {
        this.destroy()
    }

    internal inline fun <R> callWithPointer(block: (ptr: Pointer) -> R): R {
        // Check and increment the call counter, to keep the object alive.
        // This needs a compare-and-set retry loop in case of concurrent updates.
        do {
            val c = this.callCounter.get()
            if (c == 0L) {
                throw IllegalStateException("${this.javaClass.simpleName} object has already been destroyed")
            }
            if (c == Long.MAX_VALUE) {
                throw IllegalStateException("${this.javaClass.simpleName} call counter would overflow")
            }
        } while (! this.callCounter.compareAndSet(c, c + 1L))
        // Now we can safely do the method call without the pointer being freed concurrently.
        try {
            return block(this.uniffiClonePointer())
        } finally {
            // This decrement always matches the increment we performed above.
            if (this.callCounter.decrementAndGet() == 0L) {
                cleanable.clean()
            }
        }
    }

    // Use a static inner class instead of a closure so as not to accidentally
    // capture `this` as part of the cleanable's action.
    private class UniffiCleanAction(private val pointer: Pointer?) : Runnable {
        override fun run() {
            pointer?.let { ptr ->
                uniffiRustCall { status ->
                    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_free_streamifyengine(ptr, status)
                }
            }
        }
    }

    fun uniffiClonePointer(): Pointer {
        return uniffiRustCall() { status ->
            UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_clone_streamifyengine(pointer!!, status)
        }
    }

    
    /**
     * Register a push event listener (MP-C02). The registration itself
     * bumps the revision, so every listener — including the fresh one —
     * receives a full snapshot on the dispatcher's next pass. Returns
     * the number of registered listeners after the call.
     */
    @Throws(EngineException::class)override fun `addEventListener`(`listener`: EngineEventListener): kotlin.UInt {
            return FfiConverterUInt.lift(
    callWithPointer {
    uniffiRustCallWithError(EngineException) { _status ->
    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_method_streamifyengine_add_event_listener(
        it, FfiConverterTypeEngineEventListener.lower(`listener`),_status)
}
    }
    )
    }
    

    
    /**
     * Atomically apply one Jam CRDT delta batch: `delta` is a
     * concatenation of 48-byte little-endian wire ops (the frozen
     * `rust_48` image pinned by fixtures/jam_golden_vectors.json). The
     * whole batch merges under one lock acquisition; corrupt rows are
     * counted in `ops_rejected` and skipped, never aborting the batch.
     * A length that is not a multiple of 48 fails as Internal.
     */
    @Throws(EngineException::class)override fun `applyJamDelta`(`delta`: kotlin.ByteArray): JamSyncResult {
            return FfiConverterTypeJamSyncResult.lift(
    callWithPointer {
    uniffiRustCallWithError(EngineException) { _status ->
    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_method_streamifyengine_apply_jam_delta(
        it, FfiConverterByteArray.lower(`delta`),_status)
}
    }
    )
    }
    

    
    /**
     * Create the engine's active Jam session (UFFI-03/04). Creating
     * while a session is live replaces it with a fresh, empty replica
     * (the engine is the room's local mirror; a new room means a new
     * mirror). A blank or oversized name is rejected as Internal.
     */
    @Throws(EngineException::class)override fun `createJamSession`(`sessionName`: kotlin.String): JamSessionHandle {
            return FfiConverterTypeJamSessionHandle.lift(
    callWithPointer {
    uniffiRustCallWithError(EngineException) { _status ->
    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_method_streamifyengine_create_jam_session(
        it, FfiConverterString.lower(`sessionName`),_status)
}
    }
    )
    }
    

    
    /**
     * Current buffering health snapshot (one-shot read).
     */
    @Throws(EngineException::class)override fun `getBufferStats`(): BufferStats {
            return FfiConverterTypeBufferStats.lift(
    callWithPointer {
    uniffiRustCallWithError(EngineException) { _status ->
    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_method_streamifyengine_get_buffer_stats(
        it, _status)
}
    }
    )
    }
    

    
    /**
     * Latest coalesced engine snapshot (the same image pushed to
     * listeners; one-shot read for cold-start UI hydration).
     */
    @Throws(EngineException::class)override fun `getEngineSnapshot`(): EngineSnapshot {
            return FfiConverterTypeEngineSnapshot.lift(
    callWithPointer {
    uniffiRustCallWithError(EngineException) { _status ->
    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_method_streamifyengine_get_engine_snapshot(
        it, _status)
}
    }
    )
    }
    

    
    /**
     * Deterministic BLAKE3 state hash (64 hex chars) of the active
     * session's canonical CRDT fold. Requires a live session.
     */
    @Throws(EngineException::class)override fun `getJamStateHash`(): kotlin.String {
            return FfiConverterString.lift(
    callWithPointer {
    uniffiRustCallWithError(EngineException) { _status ->
    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_method_streamifyengine_get_jam_state_hash(
        it, _status)
}
    }
    )
    }
    

    
    /**
     * Current playback lifecycle state (one-shot read; UI shells should
     * prefer the push channel via `add_event_listener` — MP-DEC-04).
     */
    @Throws(EngineException::class)override fun `getPlaybackState`(): PlaybackState {
            return FfiConverterTypePlaybackState.lift(
    callWithPointer {
    uniffiRustCallWithError(EngineException) { _status ->
    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_method_streamifyengine_get_playback_state(
        it, _status)
}
    }
    )
    }
    

    
    /**
     * Arm the engine: verify runtime environment invariants, then
     * advance the lifecycle one-way Idle -> Buffering. Idempotent;
     * a progressed state is never pulled back.
     */
    @Throws(EngineException::class)override fun `initialize`()
        = 
    callWithPointer {
    uniffiRustCallWithError(EngineException) { _status ->
    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_method_streamifyengine_initialize(
        it, _status)
}
    }
    
    

    
    /**
     * Feed one reverse-plane media command (MP-C03). Applies the
     * mandated 50-150 ms hardware debounce: an IDENTICAL command
     * (same variant, same payload) arriving inside the window is
     * swallowed. Returns true when applied, false when debounced.
     */
    @Throws(EngineException::class)override fun `onMediaCommand`(`command`: MediaCommand): kotlin.Boolean {
            return FfiConverterBoolean.lift(
    callWithPointer {
    uniffiRustCallWithError(EngineException) { _status ->
    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_method_streamifyengine_on_media_command(
        it, FfiConverterTypeMediaCommand.lower(`command`),_status)
}
    }
    )
    }
    

    
    /**
     * Feed one live telemetry sample from the playback buffer and the
     * acoustic pipeline. Malformed samples (NaN/±inf fill ratio) are
     * rejected; out-of-range fill ratios clamp to [0.0, 1.0].
     */
    @Throws(EngineException::class)override fun `recordBufferStats`(`bufferedDurationMs`: kotlin.ULong, `underrunCount`: kotlin.UInt, `fillRatio`: kotlin.Float)
        = 
    callWithPointer {
    uniffiRustCallWithError(EngineException) { _status ->
    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_method_streamifyengine_record_buffer_stats(
        it, FfiConverterULong.lower(`bufferedDurationMs`),FfiConverterUInt.lower(`underrunCount`),FfiConverterFloat.lower(`fillRatio`),_status)
}
    }
    
    

    
    /**
     * Record one hardware DAC presentation clock sample (MP-C01). The
     * platform HAL adapter calls this from its callback (never the
     * engine's audio thread). The sample is validated (rate within the
     * supported DAC range) and republished in every pushed snapshot.
     */
    @Throws(EngineException::class)override fun `recordDacClock`(`clock`: DacPresentationClock)
        = 
    callWithPointer {
    uniffiRustCallWithError(EngineException) { _status ->
    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_method_streamifyengine_record_dac_clock(
        it, FfiConverterTypeDacPresentationClock.lower(`clock`),_status)
}
    }
    
    

    
    /**
     * Apply the output gain scalar. Clamped to [0.0, 1.0]; a NaN input
     * fails safe to full mute. Stored lock-free (Release/Acquire).
     */
    @Throws(EngineException::class)override fun `setVolume`(`volume`: kotlin.Float)
        = 
    callWithPointer {
    uniffiRustCallWithError(EngineException) { _status ->
    UniffiLib.INSTANCE.uniffi_streamify_contract_fn_method_streamifyengine_set_volume(
        it, FfiConverterFloat.lower(`volume`),_status)
}
    }
    
    

    

    
    
    companion object
    
}

/**
 * @suppress
 */
public object FfiConverterTypeStreamifyEngine: FfiConverter<StreamifyEngine, Pointer> {

    override fun lower(value: StreamifyEngine): Pointer {
        return value.uniffiClonePointer()
    }

    override fun lift(value: Pointer): StreamifyEngine {
        return StreamifyEngine(value)
    }

    override fun read(buf: ByteBuffer): StreamifyEngine {
        // The Rust code always writes pointers as 8 bytes, and will
        // fail to compile if they don't fit.
        return lift(Pointer(buf.getLong()))
    }

    override fun allocationSize(value: StreamifyEngine) = 8UL

    override fun write(value: StreamifyEngine, buf: ByteBuffer) {
        // The Rust code always expects pointers written as 8 bytes,
        // and will fail to compile if they don't fit.
        buf.putLong(Pointer.nativeValue(lower(value)))
    }
}



/**
 * Geometry of the audio pipeline feeding the engine.
 */
data class AudioStreamConfig (
    var `sampleRate`: kotlin.UInt, 
    var `channels`: kotlin.UByte, 
    var `bitDepth`: kotlin.UByte, 
    var `bufferFrames`: kotlin.UInt
) {
    
    companion object
}

/**
 * @suppress
 */
public object FfiConverterTypeAudioStreamConfig: FfiConverterRustBuffer<AudioStreamConfig> {
    override fun read(buf: ByteBuffer): AudioStreamConfig {
        return AudioStreamConfig(
            FfiConverterUInt.read(buf),
            FfiConverterUByte.read(buf),
            FfiConverterUByte.read(buf),
            FfiConverterUInt.read(buf),
        )
    }

    override fun allocationSize(value: AudioStreamConfig) = (
            FfiConverterUInt.allocationSize(value.`sampleRate`) +
            FfiConverterUByte.allocationSize(value.`channels`) +
            FfiConverterUByte.allocationSize(value.`bitDepth`) +
            FfiConverterUInt.allocationSize(value.`bufferFrames`)
    )

    override fun write(value: AudioStreamConfig, buf: ByteBuffer) {
            FfiConverterUInt.write(value.`sampleRate`, buf)
            FfiConverterUByte.write(value.`channels`, buf)
            FfiConverterUByte.write(value.`bitDepth`, buf)
            FfiConverterUInt.write(value.`bufferFrames`, buf)
    }
}



